The service templates in services/ whose header has no "source" line were
written for musdash.

The others were made by tools/catalog from the templates of two other
projects. services.SOURCES lists, for each, which. A template's header says
it too: "# source: coolify" or "# source: dokploy".

"source: coolify" is a template of Coolify
(https://github.com/coollabsio/coolify, templates/compose), which is
licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. A copy of that licence is in
services.APACHE-2.0.

"source: dokploy" is a blueprint of Dokploy's templates
(https://github.com/Dokploy/templates, blueprints), which are licensed under
the MIT License below.

Changed here, by tools/catalog, whose source says how and why:

- The header of comments is musdash's: a name, the catalogue's line about
  the service (the source's, cut to a sentence), where to read about it, its
  categories and its source. The source's other comments are removed.
- A Dokploy blueprint's template.toml is applied to its Compose file: the
  values Dokploy generates become musdash's magic variables
  (SERVICE_PASSWORD_*, SERVICE_BASE64_*, SERVICE_HEX_*, SERVICE_USER_*), a
  service that gets a domain gets SERVICE_FQDN_<SERVICE>_<PORT>, and a file
  Dokploy writes next to the stack becomes a "configs" entry with its
  content.
- Coolify's generated values are renamed to the musdash variable that
  generates the same kind of value (its SERVICE_BASE64_* is musdash's
  SERVICE_PASSWORD_*, its SERVICE_REALBASE64_* is SERVICE_BASE64_*), and a
  file it writes from "content:" becomes a "configs" entry.
- What only the source's platform reads is removed (ports, container_name,
  exclude_from_hc, its proxy's labels), a directory next to the Compose file
  or on the server becomes a named volume, every named volume is declared,
  an external network becomes the stack's own, and an image with no tag gets
  ":latest".
- A variable with no default gets an empty one, as both platforms start a
  stack with it; a service that does a job and ends is waited for by another
  with "service_completed_successfully"; a password written out in the file
  becomes a generated one where the file shows every place it is used.

Each service's name and logo are its owner's.

